artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the near-threshold reaction pp → ppη, in which two protons collide to produce two protons and an η meson. Existing theoretical models that successfully reproduce the energy dependence of the total cross section σ(pp → ppη) near threshold fail to reproduce the measured proton–proton invariant-mass spectrum dσ/dM_pp, which shows a characteristic enhancement at low pp relative momenta beyond what final-state interaction (FSI) factors in the dominant ³P₀ → ¹S₀ s partial wave predict. Three candidate mechanisms have been proposed:

(i) a significant η–nucleon (ηN) interaction not included in the standard production model;
(ii) a non-trivial energy dependence of the underlying ηN production operator (i.e. lifting the on-shell approximation in the production amplitude);
(iii) a sizable contribution of higher nucleon–nucleon partial waves, in particular NN P-waves, in the initial and/or final state.

The problem is to determine which combination of these mechanisms is responsible for the discrepancy, by:

(a) performing the full partial-wave decomposition of the ppη final state near threshold;
(b) using the spin-correlation (double-polarization) coefficient A_xx(pp → ppη), together with differential cross sections and the M_pp and M_pη invariant-mass distributions, to isolate quantitatively the contribution of NN P-waves;
(c) on this basis, establishing the strength of the ηN interaction and the energy dependence of the production operator required to reproduce simultaneously the total cross section, the M_pp and M_pη invariant-mass spectra, the angular distributions, and A_xx.

Provide the partial-wave decomposition and the resulting determination of (η–N interaction strength, energy dependence of the production operator, NN P-wave amplitudes) that resolves the pp → ppη invariant-mass puzzle.
